Z||000049||Harry||08-22-2000||10:07 AM||||Yes  put me in for the pair option!<BR>Thses are really nice looking and would look really good on any Fiero...||||reg||6
Z||000050||BellaFiero||08-22-2000||10:22 AM||||My car has undergone a few serious mods... scoops and a ram-air kit... I've ducted a front hood scoop to blow air into the sides and over the top of the radiator ( best I could do for now - maybe soon and with some interesting ideas I'll have it aimed straight at the fan ). It's a cobra mini-jet ( tricked it out with turn signals on the rear of the scoop, very nice ) and I mounted it with four screws through the hood before I fiberglassed it on. That thing is STUCK on there. Or so I thought. About a week later, the scoop was pulling free of the hood, from being pushed by the wind. That's after being fiberglassed and bolted-on and tightened with an air-tool. So I had to secure it with bars on the bottom of the hood, and larger washers, and re-glass it. And I put in a thick wire mesh. <BR>Basically, what I'm getting at, is that wind is a hell of a lot stronger than alot of people think it is. I know if I had used an adhesive tape on the hood scoop, it'd be lost somewhere on the highway by now.<P>Just food for thought.<P>------------------<BR>"No, no... you can't put that there, it'll be in the way of the Johnson Rod!"  ||||reg||4
Z||000051||rockcrawl||08-23-2000||09:06 PM||||Here's some pics of the first scoop out of the mold. It was quickly scuffed and painted with a spray can from the auto parts store. The screen will definately be an option, as I think it looks a whole lot better with it. The honeycomb mesh is fairly expensive (approx. $6.00/sq.ft.), so it will cost you about $5 extra for both. It can be painted to match or contrast the body color. I'll have pictures of the scoops on the car tomorow when I have more light and less rain!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scoop1_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scoop2_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scoop3.jpg"> <P>Also, because of the small size of the scoop, it is almost impossible to lay-up the fiberglass flange around the rear inner edges. For that reason, I am considering glassing in mounting studs on each corner, or making a separate flange and bonding it on after the scoop is layed up. I may make them available either way. Of course, having the studs would require drilling four 3/16" holes in each quarter panel. Anyway, what I'm saying is that they are in the final design stage, and should be ready soon. As the project nears completion, I'll create a page on my personal website to list all the options and prices.<P>One other note, what do you guys think about carbon fiber scoops? I'm talking real carbon fiber layed up in clear polished resin, not the imitation stuff. I'll make a set just to see how cool they look. Should be sweet for those who have the CF dash panels.<P>BellaFiero, I remember a post a while back about side scoops and their ram-air effect. It was stated that the slipstream extends about 12" away from the side of the car, and that there wouldn't be as much high pressure at the front of the scoop as one might think. I may be overestimating the power of 3M trim and emblem tape, but I'm hoping it will work because I really don't want to drill holes on my car.<P>Blade, Most people claim to gain some power at high speed by adding a scoop over the side air intake. I personally have never had a scoop (yet), so I can't say. On the passenger side, the scoop could be ducted to aid cooling of whatever you feel needs it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000052||86SE||08-23-2000||11:39 PM||||They look GREAT!  Can't wait to get a set!<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
